            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Great Northern Plains</strong> (Indo-Gangetic-Brahmaputra Plains) represent the youngest and most extensive aggradational alluvial lowland on Earth, separating the Himalayan mountain barrier in the north from the ancient Peninsular plateau in the south.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Tectonic Genesis & Theories of Origin</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Foredeep Theory (Eduard Suess):</strong> As the Himalayas rose, the compressive shove pushed the Tethyan strata northward, causing a colossal sag or depression (foredeep) in front of the resistant Peninsular shield.</li>\n                <li><strong>Rift Valley Hypothesis (Sir Sidney Burrard):</strong> Burrard posited that the plain was formed along a great sub-crustal rift valley between parallel fault lines. Subsequent seismic and gravimetric surveys disproved this in favor of a flexural foreland depression.</li>\n                <li><strong>Plate Tectonic Peripheral Foreland Basin:</strong> Modern geodynamics demonstrates that the heavy tectonic load of the overriding Himalayan thrust sheets caused the northern edge of the underthrusting Indian lithosphere to flex downward, creating an asymmetric depression deepest near the Himalayan Frontal Fault (HFF) and feathering out southward onto the Peninsular shield.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Dimensions and Alluvial Infill</h4>\n            <p>Extending over <strong>$2,400\text{ km}$</strong> from the Indus delta to the Ganga delta, the plain varies in width from $150\text{ km}$ in Assam to $400\text{ km}$ in the Punjab sector. Geophysical drilling indicates that the alluvial pile of alternating sand, silt, and clay beds reaches depths exceeding <strong>$1,500\text{ to }2,000\text{ meters}$</strong> before striking the crystalline Archaean basement.</p>\n\n            <h4>3. Aggradational Landforms</h4>\n            <p>The plains are exceptionally flat, with an average gradient of less than $20\text{ cm per km}$ (in the lower Ganga plain, dropping to $<10\text{ cm per km}$). Key fluvial landforms include natural levees, point bars, meander scrolls, braided bars, ox-bow lakes, and vast deltaic splays.</p>\n        ",
