            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>From the Himalayan foothills down to the deltaic mouths, the Great Northern Plains exhibit four distinct longitudinal <strong>morpho-sedimentary zones</strong>, differentiated by grain size, drainage hydrology, and soil fertility.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Bhabar Belt</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Location & Width:</strong> A narrow belt of <strong>8 to 16 km</strong> width running east-west along the foot of the Shiwalik range from the Indus to the Teesta.</li>\n                <li><strong>Lithology & Porosity:</strong> Composed of coarse unassorted alluvial fans—boulders, cobbles, pebbles, and gravels brought down by high-gradient Himalayan rivers.</li>\n                <li><strong>Hydrological Peculiarity:</strong> The porosity is so exceptionally high that smaller mountain torrents completely disappear underground into the boulder beds, flowing unseen.</li>\n                <li><strong>Economic Value:</strong> Dry, gravelly, and unsuited for agriculture except for deep-rooted forest trees.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Tarai (Terai) Belt</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Location & Width:</strong> Adjacent south of the Bhabar, spanning <strong>15 to 30 km</strong> in width. It is wider in the east (Bengal and Bihar) due to higher rainfall.</li>\n                <li><strong>Hydrology & Ecology:</strong> The underground streams of the Bhabar belt re-emerge at the surface without properly defined channels, creating a water-logged, marshy, and malarial tract.</li>\n                <li><strong>Vegetation & Reclamation:</strong> Originally blanketed in dense moist deciduous forests (Sal) and tall elephant grass. Extensively cleared post-1947 in Punjab, Haryana, and Uttarakhand (Udham Singh Nagar) for sugarcane, rice, and wheat farming.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. The Bhangar (Bangar) Plains</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Nature:</strong> Represents the <strong>older Pleistocene alluvium</strong> forming elevated river terraces above the reach of normal annual floods.</li>\n                <li><strong>Soil Characteristics:</strong> Dark, clayey-loam soils containing calcareous nodular concretions of calcium carbonate known as <strong>Kankar</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Barind Tract:</strong> The older alluvium of North Bengal is termed the <em>Barind tract</em>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>4. The Khadar (Bet) Plains</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Nature:</strong> Represents the <strong>newer, younger alluvium</strong> of the active flood plains, replenished annually with fresh layers of fertile silt during monsoon inundations.</li>\n                <li><strong>Agricultural Eminence:</strong> Highly fertile, non-calcareous fine silt and sand, ideal for intensive cultivation of paddy, wheat, sugarcane, and jute. Known locally as <strong>Bet</strong> or <em>Khadir</em> in Punjab.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>5. Specialized Edaphic Landforms: Reh and Bhur</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Reh / Kallar / Usar:</strong> Saline and alkaline white crusts formed on Bhangar soils due to capillary action induced by intensive canal over-irrigation in dry parts of UP, Punjab, and Haryana.</li>\n                <li><strong>Bhur:</strong> Wind-blown sandy ridges and mounds deposited during dry summer months along the elevated banks of the Ganga and Yamuna.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
