            "title": "The Regional Plains: Punjab-Haryana Plains & The Five Doabs",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Punjab-Haryana Plain</strong> constitutes the eastern wing of the vast Indus River alluvial basin. Flanked by the Shiwaliks in the north and the Thar Desert in the south, it spans approximately $1.75\text{ lakh sq km}$.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Five Historic Doabs of the Punjab</h4>\n            <p>A <strong>Doab</strong> (Persian: <em>Do</em> = two, <em>Ab</em> = water) refers to the fertile interfluvial tract between two rivers. Emperor Akbar systematized their names as portmanteaus of the bounding rivers:</p>\n            <table class=\"table table-bordered\">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr><th>Doab Name</th><th>Bounding Rivers</th><th>Key Districts / Significance</th></tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Bist (Jalandhar) Doab</strong></td><td>Between <strong>Beas</strong> and <strong>Satluj</strong></td><td>Jalandhar, Hoshiarpur, Kapurthala; fully in India.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Bari (Majha) Doab</strong></td><td>Between <strong>Beas</strong> and <strong>Ravi</strong></td><td>Amritsar, Gurdaspur, Lahore; historic core of Majha.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Rechna Doab</strong></td><td>Between <strong>Ravi</strong> and <strong>Chenab</strong></td><td>Sialkot, Gujranwala, Faisalabad (Pakistan).</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Chaj (Jech) Doab</strong></td><td>Between <strong>Chenab</strong> and <strong>Jhelum</strong></td><td>Gujrat, Sargodha (Pakistan).</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Sindh Sagar Doab</strong></td><td>Between <strong>Jhelum/Chenab</strong> and <strong>Indus</strong></td><td>Potwar plateau, Thal desert tract (Pakistan).</td></tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n\n            <h4>2. Geomorphic Features of the Punjab Plains</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Bet Lands:</strong> Low-lying active floodplains (Khadar) characterized by high water tables and fresh fertile silts.</li>\n                <li><strong>Dhayas:</strong> Steep, cliff-like erosional scarps (3 to 5 m high) separating the low Bet floodplains from the higher, older Bhangar upland plain.</li>\n                <li><strong>Chos of Hoshiarpur:</strong> Destructive seasonal torrential streams descending from the un-vegetated Shiwalik foothills, carving deep sandy gullies and burying agricultural fields in gravelly wash.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Haryana Plain & The Delhi Divide</h4>\n            <p>The <strong>Haryana Tract</strong> extends between the Ghaggar and Yamuna rivers. The subterranean <strong>Delhi-Aravalli Ridge</strong> and the Ambala swell form the primary hydrographic divide between the Arabian Sea (Indus) and Bay of Bengal (Ganga) drainage systems.</p>\n        ",
