            "title": "The Ganga Plains: Upper, Middle & Lower Ganga Geomorphology",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Ganga Plain</strong> covers approximately <strong>$3.75\text{ lakh sq km}$</strong>, extending across Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, and West Bengal. Based on relief, slope, and fluvial processes, it is divided into three major physiographic sub-regions:</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Upper Ganga Plain</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Extent:</strong> Covers western and central Uttar Pradesh, bounded by the Yamuna on the west and the 100 m contour line in the east.</li>\n                <li><strong>Sub-divisions:</strong> Comprises the <strong>Ganga-Yamuna Doab</strong>, the <strong>Rohilkhand Plain</strong> (north of the Ganga), and the <strong>Avadh Plain</strong> (drained by the Ghaghara and Gomti).</li>\n                <li><strong>Geomorphic Character:</strong> Moderate gradient ($25\text{ cm/km}$); dominated by stable Bhangar uplands dissected by sandy Bhurs along river banks.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Middle Ganga Plain</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Extent:</strong> Encompasses eastern Uttar Pradesh and the plains of Bihar.</li>\n                <li><strong>Sub-divisions:</strong> North of Ganga includes <em>Mithila Plain</em>, <em>Saran Plain</em>, and <em>Kosi Plain</em>; South of Ganga includes <em>Magadh Plain</em> and <em>Anga Plain</em>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Fluvial Dynamics & Hazards:</strong> Exceptionally flat terrain with an ultra-low gradient ($9\text{ to }10\text{ cm/km}$). Characterized by dynamic river avulsion (course shifting), braided rivers, and vast alluvial megafans (notably the <strong>Kosi Megafan</strong>, where the Kosi has migrated westward by $>120\text{ km}$ over 2.5 centuries).</li>\n                <li><strong>Ox-bow Lakes & Tals:</strong> Abundance of ox-bow lakes (termed <em>Mauns</em> or <em>Chaurs</em> in north Bihar like Kanwar Lake) and backwater depressions (<em>Tals</em> like Mokama Tal in south Bihar).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Lower Ganga Plain</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Extent:</strong> Stretches from the foot of the Rajmahal Hills to the Bay of Bengal, encompassing West Bengal.</li>\n                <li><strong>Sub-divisions:</strong> Includes the <em>Rarh Plain</em> (lateritic piedmont plain adjoining the Chotanagpur plateau), the <em>Barind Tract</em> (older alluvium), and the active <strong>Deltaic Plain</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Sundarbans Delta:</strong> The seaward extremity forms the world's largest mangrove delta, interlaced with tidal creeks, estuaries, and mudflats (Ganga-Brahmaputra-Meghna system).</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
