            "id": "m05",
            "title": "The Brahmaputra Valley: Riverine Islands, Braiding & Aggradation",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Brahmaputra Valley</strong> (also known as the <em>Assam Valley</em>) is an elongated alluvial lowland extending from <strong>Sadiya</strong> in the east to <strong>Dhubri</strong> in the west, spanning approximately <strong>$720\text{ km}$</strong> in length and $60\text{ to }80\text{ km}$ in width.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Structural Setting: A Ramp Valley</h4>\n            <p>Unlike the unconfined Ganga plain, the Brahmaputra Valley is a tectonic <strong>ramp valley</strong> tightly hemmed in on three sides:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>North:</strong> The towering Eastern Himalayas (Arunachal Pradesh and Bhutan).</li>\n                <li><strong>East:</strong> The Patkai Bum and Naga Hills of the Purvanchal.</li>\n                <li><strong>South:</strong> The steep crystalline scarp of the Meghalaya Plateau and Karbi Anglong Hills.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Braiding Dynamics & Sandbars (Chars)</h4>\n            <p>The Brahmaputra carries the second highest sediment discharge in the world (after the Yellow River). Heavy monsoonal runoff, combined with fine tectonic silt from steep Himalayan tributaries (Subansiri, Jia Bhareli, Manas), overloads the river's transport capacity. Consequently, the stream separates into a multi-channel network of interlacing channels separated by transient alluvial sandbars known locally as <strong>Chars</strong> or <strong>Chapories</strong>.</p>\n\n            <h4>3. Majuli Island: The Riverine Marvel</h4>\n            <p><strong>Majuli</strong> is one of the world's largest inhabited mid-river deltaic islands, situated in upper Assam between the main channel of the Brahmaputra to the south and the <strong>Kherkutia Xuti</strong> (an anabranch joined by the Subansiri) to the north:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Historical Shrinkage:</strong> Due to severe bank erosion and annual monsoon avulsion, Majuli has contracted from $\\sim 1,250\text{ sq km}$ in the early 20th century to $\\sim 400\text{ to }500\text{ sq km}$ today.</li>\n                <li><strong>Cultural & Administrative Eminence:</strong> Center of the 15th-century Assamese Neo-Vaishnavite <em>Satra</em> monastic culture initiated by <strong>Srimanta Sankardev</strong>. In 2016, it became the first river island district in India.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>4. Aggradation Post-1950 Great Assam Earthquake</h4>\n            <p>The massive <strong>1950 Assam Earthquake (M 8.6)</strong> triggered widespread landslides that dumped millions of tons of debris into the Brahmaputra and its tributaries. This catastrophically raised the riverbed by <strong>$1.5\text{ to }3\text{ meters}$</strong>, permanently reducing channel depth and exacerbating chronic annual flood disasters in the valley.</p>\n        ",
