            "id": "m06",
            "title": "The Peninsular Plateau: Geological Evolution & Stratigraphy",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Peninsular Plateau</strong> is the geological core of the Indian subcontinent. Covering approximately <strong>$16\text{ lakh sq km}$</strong> (nearly half of India's total area), it is a triangular horst block rising between $600\text{ and }900\text{ meters}$ above sea level.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Tectonic Stability & Gondwana Legacy</h4>\n            <p>Unlike the youthful, seismically active, folded Himalayas, the Peninsular Shield is a rigid <strong>cratonic shield</strong> that has escaped marine submergence or mountain-building orogenies since the Proterozoic Era. Its rocks date back to over 3.8 billion years (Archaean Eon).</p>\n\n            <h4>2. Stratigraphic Succession of the Peninsula</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Archaean Gneissic Complex:</strong> The foundational basement rocks (Bundelkhand Gneiss, Bengal Gneiss, Nilgiri Gneiss). Highly crystalline, plutonic igneous rocks metamorphosed under extreme heat and pressure.</li>\n                <li><strong>Dharwar System ($2,500–1,800\text{ mya}$):</strong> The oldest metamorphosed sedimentary rocks of India, famously exposed in the Dharwar-Bellary-Shimoga belt of Karnataka. Contains India's richest mineral wealth: iron ore (haematite/magnetite), manganese, gold (Kolar, Hutti), copper, and lead-zinc.</li>\n                <li><strong>Cuddapah System ($1,400–600\text{ mya}$):</strong> Unfossiliferous sedimentary rocks deposited in synclinal basins (crescent-shaped Cuddapah basin in Andhra Pradesh); rich in limestone, slate, barytes, and building stones.</li>\n                <li><strong>Vindhyan System:</strong> Stratified marine sedimentary rocks (sandstone, limestone, shale) stretching from Sasaram (Bihar) to Chittorgarh (Rajasthan); rich in high-grade building sandstone (used in Red Fort, Sanchi Stupa) and diamond-bearing pipes (Panna, MP).</li>\n                <li><strong>Gondwana System (Permo-Carboniferous, $300–100\text{ mya}$):</strong> Terrestrial freshwater river and lacustrine sediments deposited in faulted graben troughs (Damodar, Mahanadi, Godavari, Wardha). Contains over <strong>98% of India's commercial coal reserves</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Regional Geomorphic Tilt</h4>\n            <p>The entire Peninsular block is tilted gently towards the east and southeast. Consequently, with the exception of the Narmada and Tapti (which flow westward through tectonic rift valleys), all major peninsular rivers flow eastward into the Bay of Bengal.</p>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "The Peninsular Plateau is the oldest, largest, and most stable geological landmass of India, covering ~16 lakh sq km.",
                "Formed part of the ancient Gondwanaland supercontinent; has remained an elevated rigid shield (craton) since the Archaean Eon.",
                "Composed of Archaean Gneisses and Schists, Dharwar metamorphosed sediments (rich in metallic ores), Cuddapah-Vindhyan sedimentary basins, and Gondwana coal-bearing grabens."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "The Peninsula is bounded by the Aravallis in the northwest, Rajmahal Hills in the northeast, Gir Range in the west, and Cardamom Hills in the south. Its detached northeast extensions are the Meghalaya Plateau and Karbi Anglong. General tilt of the plateau is from west to east, dictating the course of major peninsular rivers (Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri) into the Bay of Bengal."
            ],
