            "title": "The Central Highlands: Malwa, Bundelkhand, Baghelkhand & Vindhyas",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Central Highlands</strong> form the northern tier of the Peninsular block, situated between the Narmada River rift valley in the south and the Indo-Gangetic alluvium in the north. Bounded on the northwest by the relic fold chain of the <strong>Aravallis</strong>, they gently slope toward the northeast.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Malwa Plateau</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Lithology & Drainage:</strong> An undulating plateau composed of weathered Deccan basalt lavas, rolling hills, and black soils. Drained northward by tributaries of the Yamuna: <strong>Chambal</strong>, <strong>Kali Sindh</strong>, <strong>Parbati</strong>, <strong>Betwa</strong>, and <strong>Ken</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Chambal Badlands:</strong> The lower Chambal valley in northern Malwa/Madhya Bharat features catastrophic gully erosion that has transformed fertile agricultural plains into the infamous <strong>Chambal Ravines (Bihads / Khad)</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Bundelkhand Upland</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Location & Geology:</strong> Spans northern MP and southern UP (Jhansi, Lalitpur, Jalaun, Banda, Hamirpur). Composed of the Archaean <strong>Bundelkhand Gneiss</strong>—coarse pink granites traversed by quartz reefs and dolerite dykes.</li>\n                <li><strong>Geomorphology:</strong> Senile, peneplained topography punctuated by residual granite tor hills (inselbergs), shallow rocky soils, and acute recurrent drought vulnerability.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Baghelkhand</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Located east of the Maikal range and north of the Son River trough. Composed of Vindhyan limestones and sandstones in the west and Gondwana formations in the east (Singrauli coalfield).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>4. The Vindhyan Range</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Structural Nature:</strong> An escarpment range rather than a true fold mountain, forming the northern wall of the Narmada rift valley. Extends over $1,200\text{ km}$ from Jobat (Gujarat) to Sasaram (Bihar).</li>\n                <li><strong>Sub-ranges:</strong> Includes the <strong>Kaimur Range</strong> (overlooking the Son River) and the <strong>Bhander Plateau</strong>. Functions as the absolute watershed between northern (Ganga) and southern (Peninsular) river drainage.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
