            "title": "The Chotanagpur Plateau: The Mineral Heartland ('Ruhr of India')",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Chotanagpur Plateau</strong> represents the northeastern projection of the Indian Peninsular Shield. Covering roughly <strong>$65,000\text{ sq km}$</strong> (occupying nearly the entirety of Jharkhand state along with bordering districts of West Bengal, Odisha, and Chhattisgarh), it is universally recognized as the <strong>'Ruhr of India'</strong> and India's <strong>Mineral Heartland</strong>.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Structural Components & Relief</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Pat Lands (Western Chotanagpur):</strong> Elevated plateaus rising to $1,000–1,100\text{ m}$ (e.g., Netarhat Pat, Bagru Pat) capped by thick bauxite-rich laterite crusts, representing remnants of ancient peneplains.</li>\n                <li><strong>Ranchi Plateau (Southern section):</strong> An undulating peneplain (elevation $\\sim 600\text{ m}$) featuring monadnocks (isolated granite hills) and radial drainage. The river edges feature dramatic waterfalls (e.g., <strong>Hundru Falls</strong> on Subarnarekha, <strong>Jonha Falls</strong>, <strong>Dassam Falls</strong>).</li>\n                <li><strong>Hazaribagh Plateau (Northern section):</strong> Sits north of the Damodar valley, subdivided into an upper plateau and a lower plateau (Koderma plateau, renowned for the world's richest ruby mica belt).</li>\n                <li><strong>Parasnath Peak (1,366 m):</strong> Highest mountain summit in Jharkhand, located on the eastern fringe of the Hazaribagh plateau. A sacred Jain teerth (Shikharji) where 20 of the 24 Tirthankaras attained Nirvana.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Damodar Tectonic Graben & Coalfields</h4>\n            <p>The plateau is bisected from west to east by the <strong>Damodar River</strong>, which flows through a down-faulted Gondwana structural rift valley (graben). This graben preserves India's richest prime coking and thermal coalfields: <strong>Jharia</strong> (India's primary source of metallurgical coking coal), <strong>Raniganj</strong> (birthplace of Indian coal mining, 1774), <strong>Bokaro</strong>, <strong>Karanpura</strong>, and <strong>Giridih</strong>.</p>\n\n            <h4>3. Mineral & Industrial Primacy</h4>\n            <p>Chotanagpur produces the lion's share of India's heavy industrial minerals: <strong>Iron Ore</strong> (Singhbhum iron ore belt, Noamundi, Gua), <strong>Uranium</strong> (Jaduguda, Bhatin, Narwapahar), <strong>Copper</strong> (Mosabani, Ghatsila in the Singhbhum Shear Zone), <strong>Bauxite</strong> (Lohardaga), and <strong>Mica</strong> (Koderma).</p>\n        ",
