            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Western Ghats</strong> form a formidable, nearly continuous orographic barrier along peninsular India's western flank. Overland transport between the Arabian Sea coastal ports and the Deccan interior has historically depended on a handful of strategic natural <strong>passes and structural gaps</strong>.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Major Strategic Passes of the Western Ghats</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Thalghat (Kasara Ghat, ~585 m):</strong> Located in the Northern Sahyadris of Maharashtra. Links <strong>Mumbai with Nashik</strong>, carrying National Highway 160 and the Central Railway main line toward northern and central India (Nagpur, Kolkata, Delhi).</li>\n                <li><strong>Bhorghat (Khandala Ghat, ~610 m):</strong> Located in Maharashtra between Khopoli and Khandala. Links <strong>Mumbai with Pune</strong>, carrying the Mumbai-Pune Expressway and the railway line toward Bengaluru, Chennai, and Hyderabad.</li>\n                <li><strong>Palghat Gap (Palakkad Gap, ~30 km wide, elevation ~140 m):</strong> A major physiographic break that completely severs the continuity of the Western Ghats. Located between the <strong>Nilgiri Hills</strong> in the north and the <strong>Anaimalai Hills</strong> in the south, connecting Palakkad (Kerala) with Coimbatore (Tamil Nadu).</li>\n                <li><strong>Shencottah Gap:</strong> A lower mountain break south of the Palghat, cutting through the Cardamom Hills to connect <strong>Kollam and Thiruvananthapuram</strong> (Kerala) with <strong>Tenkasi and Madurai</strong> (Tamil Nadu).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Crown Summits of the Western Ghats</h4>\n            <table class=\"table table-bordered\">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr><th>Peak</th><th>Elevation</th><th>State / Hill Range</th><th>Significance</th></tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Anamudi</strong></td><td>2,695 m</td><td>Kerala (Anaimalai Hills)</td><td>Highest peak in Peninsular India & South Asia south of Himalayas.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Meesapulimala</strong></td><td>2,640 m</td><td>Kerala (Idukki / Munnar)</td><td>Second highest peak in South India.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Doda Betta</strong></td><td>2,637 m</td><td>Tamil Nadu (Nilgiri Hills)</td><td>Highest peak in the Nilgiri plateau.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Kudremukh</strong></td><td>1,892 m</td><td>Karnataka (Chikkamagaluru)</td><td>Horse-faced peak; major iron ore deposit & National Park.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Kalsubai</strong></td><td>1,646 m</td><td>Maharashtra (Northern Sahyadris)</td><td>Highest peak in Maharashtra.</td></tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        ",
