            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>At the southern apex of peninsular India, the Western and Eastern Ghats converge into a spectacular, high-altitude highland tract known as the <strong>Southern Hill Complex</strong>.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Nilgiri Plateau ('Blue Mountains')</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Geographic Tri-junction:</strong> Situated at the meeting point of Tamil Nadu, Kerala, and Karnataka. Formed by a massive tectonic uplift (horst block) of Archaean charnockites.</li>\n                <li><strong>Relief & Summits:</strong> An undulating high plateau averaging $2,000\text{ m}$ elevation. Highest summit is <strong>Doda Betta (2,637 m)</strong>; others include Makurti (2,554 m). Home to the premier hill station <strong>Udhagamandalam (Ooty)</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Shola-Grassland Ecosystem:</strong> Unique mosaic of high-altitude montane grasslands interspersed in the sheltered valley folds by stunted, moss-laden evergreen rainforests termed <strong>Sholas</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Indigenous Peoples:</strong> Cradle of ancient pastoral tribal communities, most notably the <strong>Toda</strong> people renowned for their barrel-vaulted huts and sacred dairy temples.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Anaimalai Orographic Knot</h4>\n            <p>South of the Palghat Gap lies the towering <strong>Anaimalai Knot</strong>, from which three distinct mountain arms radiate in different directions:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Anaimalai Hills (Elephant Hills) to the North:</strong> Densely forested crystalline range hosting <strong>Anamudi (2,695 m)</strong>, the highest mountain summit in India south of the Himalayas.</li>\n                <li><strong>Palani Hills to the Northeast:</strong> An eastward spur extending into Tamil Nadu, terminating near Dindigul. Houses the famous lake hill resort of <strong>Kodaikanal (2,133 m)</strong> and the highest peak <em>Vandaravu (2,553 m)</em>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Cardamom Hills (Elaimalai) to the South:</strong> Named after the extensive cultivation of cardamom, pepper, and tea. Separated from the southern tip by the <strong>Shencottah Gap</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Agasthyamalai & The Southern Terminus</h4>\n            <p>South of the Shencottah Gap, the range continues as the <strong>Agasthyamalai Hills</strong> (highest point: <em>Agasthyamalai Peak, 1,868 m</em>), celebrated for rich medicinal plant diversity and home to the Kanikkar tribal community. The hills finally taper out into low rocky knolls near <strong>Kanyakumari (Cape Comorin)</strong>.</p>\n        ",
