            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The westernmost projection of the Indian subcontinent encompasses the peninsulas of <strong>Kutch</strong> and <strong>Kathiawar (Saurashtra)</strong> in the state of Gujarat, displaying unique arid, volcanic, and coastal geomorphic landforms.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Kutch Peninsula & The Great Rann</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Tectonic Nature:</strong> Kutch is an east-west trending horst block bounded by active strike-slip and reverse faults (Kachchh Mainland Fault, Island Belt Fault, Allah Bund Fault).</li>\n                <li><strong>The Great Rann of Kutch (~18,000 sq km):</strong> A vast, desolate expanse of glistening white hyper-saline mudflat and salt crust. During the summer monsoon, storm surges from the Arabian Sea and river runoff submerge the flat under shallow brine (1–2 m deep). In the dry season, evaporation leaves behind a blinding white crust of pure sea salt (the <em>'White Desert'</em>).</li>\n                <li><strong>The Little Rann of Kutch (~5,000 sq km):</strong> Situated southeast of the Great Rann, opening into the Gulf of Kutch. It is the sole global refuge of the endangered <strong>Indian Wild Ass (Equus hemionus khur)</strong> in the Wild Ass Sanctuary.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Allah Bund (1819 Earthquake):</strong> The devastating earthquake of 16 June 1819 (M ~7.8) created a 90 km long, 3 m high natural dam-like fault scarp across the northern Rann, christened by locals as <strong>'Allah Bund' (God's Dyke)</strong>, permanently altering the drainage of the Indus river.</li>\n                <li><strong>Banni Grasslands:</strong> Formed by alluvium deposited by the Bhukhi and other rivers on the southern edge of the Great Rann, covering ~2,500 sq km of saline-tolerant grasslands with distinctive circular mud huts called <em>Bhongas</em>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Kathiawar (Saurashtra) Peninsula</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Volcanic Core & Relief:</strong> Formed largely of Cretaceous Deccan basalt trap lavas. The central highland consists of the <strong>Mandav Hills</strong> and the volcanic ring-complex of <strong>Mount Girnar (1,117 m)</strong>, the highest peak in Gujarat.</li>\n                <li><strong>Radial Drainage Pattern:</strong> Rivers originate in the central volcanic uplands and flow outward in all directions like spokes of a wheel: <em>Bhadar</em> (flowing west into the Arabian Sea), <em>Shetrunji</em> (east into Gulf of Khambhat), and <em>Bhogavo</em> (north into Little Rann).</li>\n                <li><strong>Gir National Park:</strong> Located in the Gir Hills of southern Saurashtra, it is the <strong>last and only natural sanctuary of the Asiatic Lion (Panthera leo persica)</strong> in the wild.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
