            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Western Coastal Plain</strong> is an elongated, narrow littoral strip stretching approximately <strong>$1,500\text{ km}$</strong> between the precipitous western face of the Sahyadris and the Arabian Sea. It is divided into four distinct regional sectors from north to south:</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Regional Sectors of the Western Coast</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Gujarat Coastal Plain:</strong> The widest sector, formed by the alluvial deposits of the Narmada, Tapti, Mahi, and Sabarmati rivers around the Gulf of Khambhat.</li>\n                <li><strong>Konkan Coast (Daman to Goa, ~500 km):</strong> Characterized by a rugged, indented shoreline with cliffed rocky headlands, submerged sea-caves, and deep ria estuaries (Ulhas, Savitri, Shastri, Mandovi, Zuari). Mumbai and Mormugao are situated here.</li>\n                <li><strong>Kannad (Canara / Karnataka) Coast (Goa to Mangalore, ~225 km):</strong> Narrow and sandy, backed by steep forest-clad hills. Traversed by short, torrential, waterfall-forming rivers like the <strong>Sharavati</strong> (creating the world-famous <em>Jog / Gersoppa Falls</em>, drop 253 m).</li>\n                <li><strong>Malabar Coast (Mangalore to Kanyakumari, ~550 km):</strong> Wider and characterized by coastal dunes, mud banks (<em>Chakara</em>), and extensive systems of backwaters known locally as <strong>Kayals</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Kayals & Lagoons of Kerala</h4>\n            <p>The Malabar coast features an interconnected network of brackish lagoons, estuaries, and barrier sandbars running parallel to the sea:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Vembanad Kayal (Vembanad Lake):</strong> The <strong>longest lake in India (~96.5 km long)</strong>, covering over $2,000\text{ sq km}$ of wetlands. Divided into freshwater and brackish zones by the <em>Thanneermukkom Salt Water Barrier</em>. Hosts the world-renowned <strong>Nehru Trophy Boat Race (Vallam Kali)</strong> at Punnamada, Alappuzha.</li>\n                <li><strong>Ashtamudi Kayal:</strong> A palm-shaped Ramsar wetland in Kollam with eight prominent arms (Ashta = eight, Mudi = hair/braid), connecting to the sea through the Neendakara bar.</li>\n                <li><strong>Kuttanad Region:</strong> The 'Rice Bowl of Kerala', renowned for farming paddies at <strong>$1.2\text{ to }3\text{ meters below mean sea level}$</strong> using bio-saline dyke management. Designated by FAO as a <em>Globally Important Agricultural Heritage System (GIAHS)</em>.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
