            "title": "The Eastern Coastal Plain: Deltas, Circars & Coromandel Coast",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Eastern Coastal Plain</strong> is a broad, gently sloping littoral lowland stretching between the Eastern Ghats and the Bay of Bengal from the Subarnarekha River down to Kanyakumari. It averages <strong>$80\text{ to }150\text{ km}$</strong> in width—vastly wider than its western counterpart.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Regional Subdivisions</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Utkal Plain (Odisha Coast, ~400 km):</strong> Extends from the Subarnarekha to the Rushikulya river. Formed by the deltaic deposits of the <strong>Mahanadi</strong>, <strong>Brahmani</strong>, and <strong>Baitarani</strong> rivers. Houses <strong>Chilika Lake</strong>, India's largest coastal lagoon.</li>\n                <li><strong>Northern Circars (Andhra Coast):</strong> Stretches between the Mahanadi and Krishna deltas, encompassing the coalescing <strong>Godavari and Krishna Deltas</strong>. Between these two active delta lobes lies the shallow freshwater depression of <strong>Kolleru Lake</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Coromandel Coast (Tamil Nadu Coast):</strong> Stretches from the Krishna delta (or Point Calimere) down to Kanyakumari. Dominated by the <strong>Kaveri Delta</strong> (celebrated as the <em>'Granary of South India'</em>). Receives maximum precipitation in <strong>October–December</strong> from the Northeast (Retreating) Monsoon.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Coastal Deltas & Lagoons</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Chilika Lake (Odisha):</strong> Pear-shaped brackish water lagoon (~1,100 sq km) designated as India's first Ramsar site (1981). Connected to the Bay of Bengal via a narrow spit. Winter sanctuary for over a million migratory birds and home to the endangered <strong>Irrawaddy Dolphin (Orcaella brevirostris)</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Pulicat Lake:</strong> Second largest brackish lagoon in India (~759 sq km), straddling the Andhra Pradesh-Tamil Nadu border. Protected by the barrier island of <strong>Sriharikota</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Kolleru Lake:</strong> One of India's largest natural freshwater lakes, situated in Andhra Pradesh between the Krishna and Godavari river basins; Ramsar wetland and bird sanctuary.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
