            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Andaman and Nicobar Archipelago</strong> consists of an arcuate chain of <strong>572 islands, islets, and rocks</strong> stretching over $800\text{ km}$ between $6^\\circ 45'\text{ N}$ and $13^\\circ 45'\text{ N}$ latitude in the southeastern Bay of Bengal. Covering $8,249\text{ sq km}$, this Union Territory possesses supreme strategic, ecological, and maritime significance.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Tectonic Genesis & Lithology</h4>\n            <p>The islands are <em>not</em> coral atolls; they represent the elevated subaerial peaks of a tertiary <strong>submerged fold-mountain arc</strong> that connects the <strong>Arakan Yoma</strong> of Myanmar southward to the Mentawai ridge and Sumatra (Indonesia). This collision zone between the Indo-Australian plate and the Burma micro-plate preserves authentic <strong>ophiolite suites</strong> (slices of ancient oceanic crust and upper mantle obducted onto the continental margin).</p>\n\n            <h4>2. Structural Zonation & Key Maritime Channels</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Andaman Group:</strong> Comprises North Andaman, Middle Andaman (largest island in India by area), South Andaman (location of capital <em>Port Blair</em> / Sri Vijaya Puram), Baratang, and Little Andaman.\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Duncan Passage:</strong> The maritime strait separating <strong>South Andaman from Little Andaman</strong>.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Saddle Peak (732 m):</strong> The highest mountain summit in the entire archipelago, located in North Andaman within Saddle Peak National Park.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>The Ten Degree Channel:</strong> A $150\text{ km}$ wide oceanic waterway lying along the <strong>$10^\\circ\text{ N}$ parallel</strong>, which formally separates the Andaman Group in the north from the Nicobar Group in the south.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Nicobar Group:</strong> Comprises Car Nicobar (northernmost), Camorta, Katchal, Nancowry, Little Nicobar, and <strong>Great Nicobar</strong> (largest of the group).\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Indira Point ($6^\\circ 45'\text{ N}$):</strong> Southernmost tip of the Indian Union on Great Nicobar, overlooking the strategic Six Degree Channel (Great Channel) leading to the Malacca Strait.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Mount Thullier (642 m):</strong> Highest peak in the Nicobar group (on Great Nicobar).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Volcanic Manifestations</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Barren Island:</strong> Situated ~135 km northeast of Port Blair; the <strong>only confirmed active volcano in India and South Asia</strong> (erupted in 1991, 2005, and 2017+).</li>\n                <li><strong>Narcondam Island:</strong> A conical, heavily forested volcanic island ~160 km north of Barren Island; classified as a <strong>dormant volcano</strong>, famous for the endemic Narcondam Hornbill.</li>\n                <li><strong>Baratang Mud Volcanoes:</strong> India's only known active terrestrial mud volcanoes, bubbling with methane gas and cold sediment slurry.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
