            "title": "Lakshadweep Archipelago: Coral Atolls, Channels & Marine Ecology",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Lakshadweep Archipelago</strong> is an idyllic chain of <strong>36 islands, atolls, submerged coral banks, and reefs</strong> scattered across the Arabian Sea between $8^\\circ\text{ N}$ and $12^\\circ\text{ N}$ latitude, roughly $200\text{ to }440\text{ km}$ off the coast of Kerala. With a total land area of merely <strong>$32\text{ sq km}$</strong>, it is India's smallest Union Territory.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Biogenic Origin: Coral Atolls on a Volcanic Ridge</h4>\n            <p>Unlike the tectonic fold origin of the Andaman & Nicobar islands, Lakshadweep is <strong>100% biogenic (coral origin)</strong>. The islands represent living and fossilized <strong>coral atolls</strong> (circular or horseshoe-shaped reefs enclosing a central turquoise lagoon) resting atop the basaltic peaks of the <strong>Chagos-Laccadive Submarine Ridge</strong>, formed as the Indian plate moved over the Reunion hotspot.</p>\n\n            <h4>2. Morphological Groups & Strategic Channels</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Amindivi Islands:</strong> The northernmost group, comprising Amini, Chetlat, Kiltan, Kadmat, and Bitra (smallest inhabited island).</li>\n                <li><strong>Laccadive (Cannanore) Islands:</strong> The central group, comprising Andrott (largest island by land area, 4.90 sq km), <strong>Kavaratti</strong> (administrative capital), Agatti (location of the sole commercial airport), and Kalpeni.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Nine Degree Channel (9° N):</strong> A major international shipping corridor (~200 km wide) separating the <strong>Minicoy Atoll in the south from the central Lakshadweep group (Kalpeni and Suheli)</strong> in the north.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Eight Degree Channel (8° N):</strong> Marks the maritime international boundary separating <strong>Minicoy Island (India)</strong> from the northernmost atolls of the <strong>Republic of Maldives</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Minicoy Island (Maliku):</strong> The southernmost and second largest island (4.80 sq km). Features a unique crescent shape and a deep lagoon. Culturally distinct from the rest of Lakshadweep: the population speaks <em>Mahal</em> (a dialect of Maldivian Dhivehi) and follows a matrilineal social order.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Marine Ecosystem & Vulnerabilities</h4>\n            <p>The islands are surrounded by shallow, wave-calming lagoons supporting rich marine biodiversity (staghorn corals, sea cucumbers, giant clams, green turtles, and tuna fisheries). <strong>Pitti Island</strong> is an uninhabited sandbank designated as a Bird Sanctuary, hosting pelagic nesting terns. Because maximum elevation is only <strong>$3\text{ to }5\text{ meters}$ above sea level</strong>, the entire archipelago faces grave risks from coral bleaching and sea-level rise.</p>\n        ",
